Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can use the `PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation` helper to wire up a **read‑only, multi‑line** text area that supports both horizontal and vertical scrolling.  
The example assumes you already have a `IResultSetController` instance called `dataController` and a SWT `Composite` called `compositeParent`.

```java
import org.eclipse.swt.SWT;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Composite;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Text;
import org.eclipse.swt.layout.FillLayout;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Create the plain‑text presentation
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object presentation = functions.PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation(
        new Object[]{ 
            /* controller */ "dataController",
            /* parent     */ "compositeParent"
        }
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Retrieve the underlying Text widget (if needed)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Text textArea = null;
if (presentation instanceof Text) {
    textArea = (Text) presentation;
} else if (presentation instanceof Composite) {
    // If the helper returns a composite that contains the Text widget,
    // you can locate it by traversing children or by storing a reference
    // during creation. For simplicity, assume it returns the Text itself.
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Configure the Text widget
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
if (textArea != null) {
    // Make it read‑only
    textArea.setEditable(false);

    // Enable multi‑line support
    textArea.setTextLimit(0);          // unlimited characters
    textArea.setLinesVisible(true);    // show line numbers if desired

    // Add scroll bars
    textArea.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL);

    // Optional: set layout data so it expands to fill the parent
    textArea.setLayoutData(new FillLayout());
}
```

### What the helper does

* **Creates a `Text` widget** inside the supplied `Composite` (`compositeParent`).
* Sets the widget’s style flags to include:
  * `SWT.MULTI` – allows multiple lines.
  * `SWT.READ_ONLY` – makes the widget non‑editable.
  * `SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL` – adds vertical and horizontal scroll bars.
* Adds a listener that forwards changes from the `IResultSetController` to the text area (so the view stays in sync with the underlying data).

### Why this works

* `SWT.MULTI` + `SWT.READ_ONLY` → read‑only, multi‑line text area.
* `SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL` → both scroll bars appear automatically when content overflows.
* The helper returns the created widget (or a composite containing it), so you can further customize it as shown.

Feel free to adapt the code to your exact environment (e.g., if the helper returns a composite instead of the `Text` widget directly).
